WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.640
Close one eye of a young kitten for a week, and its visual cortex changes.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.960
Close an adult cat's eye for months, and the cortex looks normal.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.700
Why?

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:17.723
To see what changed, follow the signal from the eyes to the cortex.

5
00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:24.077
Each eye sends its axons to a relay station in the thalamus, the lateral geniculate

6
00:00:24.077 --> 00:00:26.557
nucleus. We will call it the LGN.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.740
There the two eyes' axons end in separate layers.

8
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.920
So most LGN cells are driven by one eye.

9
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:40.380
LGN axons carry the signal on to the primary visual cortex, at the back of the

10
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:41.380
brain.

11
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:43.463
They end mainly in layer 4.

12
00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:47.663
There the two eyes' endings still lie apart, in alternating stripes.

13
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:53.133
In macaque monkeys these stripes, called ocular dominance columns, are sharp.

14
00:00:53.993 --> 00:00:55.913
In cats the pattern is less regular.

15
00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:00.927
In macaque layer 4C, most cells are driven mainly by one eye.

16
00:01:01.947 --> 00:01:04.107
Cat layer 4 has more cells that hear both.

17
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:09.157
Above and below layer 4, cortical neurons combine the two streams.

18
00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:12.457
Many cells there can be driven by both eyes.

19
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:18.350
In cats and monkeys, this is the first place where many single cells are driven by

20
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:19.350
both eyes.

21
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.840
The eyes compete for territory in layer 4, and for those shared cells.

22
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.310
Which eye is which depends on where you record.

23
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.310
We will always record from the left hemisphere.

24
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.300
The eye on the opposite side, the right eye, is contralateral, in orange.

25
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.200
The left eye, on the same side, is ipsilateral, in blue.

26
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.560
David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel measured the balance one cell at a time.

27
00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:50.453
A fine metal electrode sits next to one neuron and records its spikes from outside

28
00:01:50.453 --> 00:01:51.453
the cell.

29
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:56.507
With the animal anaesthetized, they showed a bar of light to one eye, with the other

30
00:01:56.507 --> 00:01:58.487
covered. Then they switched.

31
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.040
Each cell went into one of seven ocular dominance groups.

32
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:07.010
Group 1 answers only to the contralateral eye.

33
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.390
Group 7 answers only to the ipsilateral eye.

34
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.690
Group 4 answers about equally to both.

35
00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.870
The groups between favor one eye, more or less.

36
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:21.433
One worked cell fires to the right eye and not at all to the left.

37
00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:23.613
That puts it in group 1.

38
00:02:24.516 --> 00:02:27.856
A cell that fires only to the left eye goes in group 7.

39
00:02:28.656 --> 00:02:31.596
A cell no eye can drive is counted as unresponsive.

40
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.800
Count the cells in each group, and you get an ocular dominance histogram.

41
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.820
In a normal adult cat it is a hump.

42
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.440
Most cells sit in the middle groups, leaning toward the contralateral side.

43
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:50.293
Takeaway: the eyes' paths stay apart until the cortex, where shared cells can be

44
00:02:50.293 --> 00:02:51.293
driven by both.

45
00:02:52.016 --> 00:02:53.656
So what sets each cell's group?

46
00:02:54.456 --> 00:02:57.676
The next chapter closes one eye and watches the balance move.
